aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399384 1072 #uuid "ba6bcd83-87df-5c0b-b121-63a0ff1205ca" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399384 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is **not** in row echelon form." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399386 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399387 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399388 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399389 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399390 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399389 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399385 1080 #uuid "8